6 Tests You Can Do Yourself
Use more than one test. Together, they give a better picture.
Marks of Authentic TPU Film
Authentic TPU usually has a known brand, a spec sheet that says TPU, and a warranty from the maker. It stretches without trouble, stays clear, and heals light scratches with warmth. Ask the installer for the roll label, the data sheet, and a sample.
Brand and model check. Ask to see the roll or box. If no brand is visible, be wary.
Data sheet check. Look for TPU, the thickness, and warranty terms.
Warranty check. It should cover yellowing, cracking, and peeling. Confirm with the brand.
Scratch and warm test. Lightly scratch the sample and warm it. TPU heals, while TPH often does not.
Stretch test. TPU stretches and returns to shape. A stiffer feel suggests TPH or PVC.
Clarity test. View the sample on a dark panel under bright light. It should not look hazy or yellow.
Telltale Signs of TPH Film
You can often catch TPH by its red flags:
A price much lower than the other quotes
No brand on the roll, or vague talk about the material
A foggy or yellow-looking tint
A stiff feel with little or no self-healing
A short warranty, or no manufacturer warranty
Do not trust packaging on its own. Verify with the manufacturer when you can.
What If the Film Is Already on Your Car?
You cannot judge it well by eye. Look at your invoice and warranty card for the brand name. Then ask the manufacturer to verify it. If you see yellowing or cracking later, the film was likely lower grade.
